Z||000000||Phoenix||03-06-2000||10:55 PM||||I have just purchased an 88 coupe with 51000 miles on it. It has some minor damage on the left front head light bucket but other than that the car seems to be ok. As part of the "total package" it came with all of the body pannels from an 87 GT and the V6 also. The seller told me it has the "Lotus" suspension option, what exactly was this and how can you tell if it realy does have it.||||reg||10
Z||000001||fiero56||03-06-2000||11:10 PM||||Lotus suspension=myth, pure and simple.<P>GM did redesign the entire suspension in '88, so you do have an improved suspension from previous years, but is not by Lotus. Also, brakes in '88 are vented, supposed to be much better than previous years. All in all, everything came together for the Fiero in '88. The redesigned suspension was designed after a Lotus suspension, but noting else. It seems to be a very popular myth, but is a myth none the less.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||03-07-2000||03:25 AM||||actually, it wasn't a redesigned suspension, it was what the Fiero was supposed to have right from the start.  GM beancounting killed it in 84. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||03-07-2000||05:24 AM||||I'd hate to get in the middle of a historic disagreement, but the '88 suspension is a redesign of a Lotus suspension.  It wasn't exactly what the designers had in mind in '84, but very close.  I guess you could say that the designers redesigned the redisign.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phoenix||03-07-2000||11:54 AM||||Thanks, make sense. <BR>Next question - what's a good source for chassis dimensions? Since I will be sectioning in the left front corner sheet metal I need accurate drawings or numbers.<BR>Pontiac's servive manual S-8810-P is out of print (Helminc.com) and they couldn't tell me if it will be available again.<P>Phoenix<BR>"It will rise again"||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||03-07-2000||05:35 PM||||What sheet metal?  The body panels are Enduroflex (a form of SMC).  Try an MVMA manual.  That might help.  I have no idea where to get one.  Someone kindly donated a copy to me.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula||03-07-2000||08:29 PM||||The 88 coupe needs stiffer springs and a sway bar to be the same as a formula/GT.||||reg||1
Z||000007||86SE||03-07-2000||09:25 PM||||I love the Enduroflex body panels!!  I hit a curb and the bumper got smashed in, then poped right back out to take its original shape with no cracks!!  Lucky me.......||||reg||14
Z||000008||Standard||03-08-2000||01:37 AM||||Enduraflex rules.. if somebody doesn't think so I just give my door a good kick and then they see why it rules..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Monkey - I've never heard that.  where'd you get the info?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>Just so all us 84-87 owners can feel a little better.. did ya know that the front suspension on them things was originally desinged by Opel in the '70's, for some form of racing.. did very well, too, sfaik.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phoenix||03-08-2000||08:04 AM||||When I said sheet metal I was refering to the<BR>stamped metal chassis parts.  As for the manual I found the complete service manual on microfiche for $25.00 when I did another search at helminc.com.  The local libraries have microfiche readers with built in printers so you can get hard copy of the section you need to take home.||||reg||1
Z||000010||william_thorniii||03-08-2000||03:04 PM||||Phoenix,<P>I live in Dayton, OH.  We have a huge Industrial Discounter store called Mendelson's.  They have well over 100 used microfiche readers.  The prices range from $25-$100.  If you would like, I could try to pick one up for you and have it shipped.  I was thinking about getting one for the garage, since I don't want to get my manuals greasy.  <P>William T. Thorn, III<P>||||reg||1
